COMMITTED HERE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

worker_stand.ply, worker_walk.ply
  Derived from "Worker", by Quaternius
  CC0 1.0 (public domain dedication)
  https://poly.pizza/m/Yg2bQZO6Hj
  https://static.poly.pizza/3a5f3056-ffe6-42eb-bd52-122afcbd22b2.glb

  Rigged and skinned, with 24 baked animations and the hard hat and hi-vis
  vest on their own materials. Stylised low-poly and flat-coloured rather
  than photoreal, which makes every detection number measured on it a lower
  bound: a scanned human should do at least as well.

  What was applied, by tools/bake_worker.py:
    - imported the GLB, deleted the shadow-blob sphere the pack ships with
      (it would be a floating ball in the LiDAR return);
    - posed the armature with the `Idle` action for `worker_stand` and `Walk`
      for `worker_walk`, sampled at frame 12 rather than rest, because rest
      is a stance that reads as neither standing nor walking;
    - evaluated the modifier stack to world-space triangles, 5240 each;
    - rotated +90 degrees about z, because the asset faces -y and sitegen
      measures yaw from +x;
    - scaled uniformly to 1.75 m tall -- actors.WORKER, the height of the box
      it replaces -- and moved so the feet sit on z = 0 at the origin.

  Material *names* survive the bake and the triangle material indices with
  them; the colours are re-declared in cycles.PALETTE. Everything else about
  the source asset is discarded.

Every other actor mesh -- both excavator ends, the boom chain, the bucket,
both units of the hauler, the grade stakes -- is procedural, built from
extruded convex profiles in meshes.py. That is a deviation from the plan and
the reason is in docs/RENDERING.md: no CC0 excavator or dump truck appears to
exist, and 3D-ConHE is not reachable.

